This diagram is intentionally simplified and does not describe the private deployment topology.
Core Capabilities
The system is designed around three practical workflows:
1. Knowledge Search
- Find relevant information in explicitly approved local project materials.
- Combine lexical/exact matching with semantic retrieval for different query types.
- Use source references and scoped retrieval rather than unsupported generated assertions.
- Distinguish missing, stale, or uncertain information from verified facts.
2. Project Re-entry
- Bring together the available project mission, current state, decisions, and known blockers.
- Help an engineer or AI-assisted development tool resume work without reconstructing every earlier discussion.
- Preserve uncertainty where source freshness or completeness cannot be confirmed.
Current maturity: the re-entry workflow is partially validated and remains subject to additional completeness checks.
3. Evidence-Assisted Review
- Organize available project requirements and supporting technical evidence for review.
- Highlight missing or conflicting information instead of inventing a passing result.
- Support engineering decisions without replacing deterministic tests or human approval.
Current maturity: evidence review is advisory and can return REVIEW_REQUIRED; it does not authorize production releases.
Local-First Engineering Approach
KIS is developed around a small set of operating principles:
- Reuse before expansion: build on existing AI Hub components instead of creating unnecessary duplicate services.
- Local-first processing: use local retrieval and model infrastructure as the default path.
- Sources of truth remain external: the search index is derived data, not an authoritative replacement for original project files.
- Least-privilege ingestion: process only approved materials within explicit source boundaries.
- Evidence before conclusions: distinguish documented claims from verified test and runtime evidence.
- Fail safely on uncertainty: missing or stale evidence calls for review, not an invented answer.
- Human control: consequential infrastructure changes and release decisions remain subject to separate approval gates.
- Measured development: prioritize working end-to-end scenarios and regression evidence over feature accumulation.
Cloud AI APIs, automatic external fallbacks, unrestricted autonomous agents, and new infrastructure services are not required for the core local workflow.
Representative Technology
The implementation is developed within the existing AIAQ self-hosted AI ecosystem.

Publicly disclosed technology categories:

Python · Local LLMs · Ollama · Open WebUI / Chroma · Hybrid Retrieval · Markdown · Git · CLI · Automated Testing · AI-assisted Engineering

The validated retrieval path reuses the existing Open WebUI / Chroma environment alongside local lexical search. This project does not claim a separate new production vector database or a publicly available SaaS endpoint.

Detailed configurations, indexing contracts, storage locations, private integration points, and production source code remain confidential.
Engineering Validation
Selected results from the internal validation snapshot recorded on November 19, 2026:
Area	Reported validation result
Retrieval foundation	Existing Open WebUI / Chroma-based approach selected and exercised
Controlled pilot corpus	6 documents / 165 chunks; 4 pilot documents
Search workflow	Scoped live retrieval verified in the local environment
Automated regression suite	145 / 145 checks passed in the recorded baseline
Primary user scenarios	3 / 3 scenarios accepted for the limited local MVP
Recovery	Controlled backup / restore checks passed during development


These figures describe bounded internal validation, not third-party certification, an availability SLA, or a claim of independently audited enterprise-wide deployment. No unverified percentage of time saved or commercial ROI is asserted.
Current Project Status
Internal operational MVP — limited owner-approved local use.

KIS has passed its recorded local regression and scoped end-to-end checks. However, the engineering record does not establish full, unrestricted Production GO.

The remaining validation boundaries include server-index freshness, completeness of project re-entry, evidence-review maturity, retrieval relevance improvements, and sustained operational stability. Advanced workflows remain review-gated while these areas are strengthened.

The system is being improved through incremental, evidence-driven releases rather than treating every planned capability as already deployed.
Public Repository Scope
Included here:
- Project purpose and operational problem.
- Simplified architectural and workflow descriptions.
- Publicly shareable technology categories.
- Selected, bounded validation milestones.
- A high-level development changelog.
Not included here:
- Private production source code or deployment manifests.
- Internal documents, customer data, raw indexed content, or embeddings.
- Credentials, tokens, secrets, or environment files.
- Private repository locations, network topology, or security configuration.
- Proprietary prompts, scoring rules, database structures, and internal operational logs.
This repository is a portfolio case study, not a ready-to-deploy open-source package.
